fat_parent is a native DOS regression for FAT directory renames.

Build explicitly with:
  make test-fat-parent-rename

On an expendable mounted FAT volume TEST:, run:
  fat_parent ROOT TEST:FatParent

The program creates the first unused TEST:FatParent-1 through -8 fixture,
plus its -Moved and -Root siblings. It does not overwrite existing objects
or remove the resulting fixtures. ROOT must resolve directly under the
volume root. The volume node must identify FAT12, FAT16 or FAT32; DOS's
generic Info.id_DiskType alone cannot distinguish FAT from other handlers.

It checks full NameFromLock paths and ParentDir identity after moving a
directory from staging to the volume root, then to another parent and back.
It also checks rejection of a cycle, replacement of a different existing
directory with the same leaf name, and use of a file as a parent. A case-only
rename of the same entry remains allowed. A preceding parent or refusal
failure stops the program before further potentially invalid renames.

BASELINE is only for observing the older handler on an expendable volume:
  fat_parent ROOT TEST:FatParent BASELINE
It expects ParentDir error 205 and an incomplete NameFromLock path after the
first cross-parent rename, then stops before the refusal probes.

Each invocation returns 0 only when its checks pass and prints a final
"fat_parent: N checks, M failed" summary. Run Shell and Run invocations
separately and retain both return codes and their completion markers.
For FAT32, the moved directory's final parent link is encoded as zero when
it is directly in the volume root; it can be inspected in a stopped image.
